CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure tracks a dataset's representation through a vision transformer as a vertical stack of five flat square planes in isometric perspective, with an architecture legend up the left side. The legend alternates upward-pointing arrows with four rounded boxes, each reading ViT block x3, so consecutive planes are separated by three transformer blocks. Every plane carries the same set of plotted points, each colored by its semantic class in roughly ten hues including teal, blue, olive, green, orange, red, brown, purple, and pink. Dashed curves in matching colors connect each point to its counterpart on the plane above, so one image can be followed through the whole stack. On the bottom plane the colors are thoroughly mixed within a narrow band; the middle planes remain largely intermingled; and by the top plane the points have spread out into separate compact clumps, each a single color. Position encodes the projected embedding and color encodes class, so what the reader compares plane to plane is how tangled the colors are. The coordinates come from a t-SNE projection computed jointly across the layers shown, giving a rough view of how depth disentangles the classes.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - five flat square planes drawn as isometric diamonds, stacked vertically with equal spacing (bottom = patch embedding output, top = final ViT output)
  - dark grey outline (~2px) on each plane's four edges
  - faint light-grey crosshair grid lines (two axes) drawn on the interior of each plane
  - ~200 scatter points per plane, colored by semantic class (about 10 classes)
  - dashed colored curves connecting each point to its counterpart on the plane immediately above, one continuous dashed trajectory per data item through all five planes
  - dense narrow band of thoroughly mixed colored points on the bottom plane
  - partially mixed point distributions on the three intermediate planes
  - ten separated single-color compact clumps spread across the top plane
  - left-hand architecture legend column: four rounded-rectangle boxes labeled 'ViT block x3'
  - five upward-pointing black arrows in the legend column, alternating with the boxes so each box sits between the plane below and the plane above it
  - thin black outlines on the rounded legend boxes, monospace label text

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Stack axis is +Y (screen-up). Each plane is a flat square 6 × 0 × 6 world units lying in the XZ plane (+X screen-right, +Z the receding diagonal), drawn as an isometric diamond with its corners pointing left/right/near/far. Planes at y = 0, 3, 6, 9, 12 (equal 3-unit gaps); total stack height 12, so the figure is roughly 2x taller than wide. Plane outline: dark grey #4a4a4a, ~2px. Interior crosshairs: light grey #e0e0e0 along the two plane diagonals-through-center (i.e. axis-aligned X and Z lines through the plane center). ~200 points total, 10 classes of 20 points each; point radius ~0.06 world units (small filled discs). Class palette read off the source: teal #2ec4c4, blue #2f7fd0, olive #b5bd35, green #33b464, orange #f57f20, red #e03030, brown #8a6a5a, grey-purple #9b8ec4, magenta-pink #e84f9a, light-pink #f0a0c0. Layout of the point sets (read off the source, do not randomize): BOTTOM plane (y=0) — all ten classes packed into a single narrow band ~3.5 units long running left-to-right across the plane center, band thickness only ~0.5 units, colors fully interleaved. PLANES y=3 and y=6 — band widens to ~4.5 × 1.2 units, still centered, colors mostly intermingled with only slight same-color grouping; on the y=6 plane the cloud sits slightly toward the near/left half. PLANE y=9 — clusters begin separating, spread ~5 × 1.5 units, same-color points visibly grouped but adjacent groups touching. TOP plane (y=12) — ten compact clumps (each ~0.6 units across) arranged in a shallow arc across the far half of the plane, ordered left-to-right roughly: blue, olive, green, teal, orange, red, brown, grey-purple, magenta, light-pink; clumps separated by clear white gaps. Dashed connectors: one cubic curve per data point from plane k to plane k+1, matching the point's class color, dash pattern ~0.12/0.10, line width ~1px, opacity ~0.35 when unhighlighted (curves passing behind a plane are drawn faded/whitened) and ~0.95 when its class is hovered; curves bow outward slightly in XZ (control points offset ~0.3 units) so the bundle reads as a waist that flares near the top. Position interpolation between planes: p(k) = lerp(bottomPos, topPos, e(k)) with e = [0, 0.12, 0.30, 0.62, 1.0] so most of the disentangling visibly happens in the top two gaps. Legend column at x ≈ -8, spanning y = 0 to 12: four rounded rectangles 4.5 wide × 1.4 tall (corner radius 0.7, white fill, black 2px stroke) centered at y = 1.5, 4.5, 7.5, 10.5, each with monospace black text 'ViT block x3'; five black arrows (shaft width ~0.1, head ~0.45 wide) centered at y = 0.3, 3, 6, 9, 12 pointing up, drawn between/above the boxes so each box sits between the two planes it maps to.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "depthSlider",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Network depth (plane index)",
    "range": [
      0,
      4,
      1
    ],
    "default": 0,
    "teaches": "Sweeping from the embedding plane to the final plane shows the colored point cloud going from one tangled band to ten separated single-color clumps — depth progressively disentangles semantic classes."
  },
  {
    "id": "classHover",
    "type": "hover",
    "teaches": "Hovering one class color brightens that class's points and dashed paths on every plane at once, making the trajectory of a single semantic class through the whole network legible inside an otherwise dense figure."
  },
  {
    "id": "blockStage",
    "type": "state",
    "label": "Step through ViT block groups",
    "states": [
      "embedding",
      "blocks 1-3",
      "blocks 4-6",
      "blocks 7-9",
      "blocks 10-12"
    ],
    "default": "embedding",
    "teaches": "Advancing one 'ViT block x3' stage at a time highlights the target plane and the incoming dashed paths, showing that class separation accumulates gradually across repeated transformer blocks rather than appearing in one layer."
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitStack",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating the stack reveals that each plane is a genuine 2D projection viewed obliquely, so apparent overlaps between clusters are a viewpoint artifact and true separation only reads once the plane is turned."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Patch embeddings (bottom plane)",
    "narration": "At the output of patch embedding, all ten classes occupy one narrow band — colors are completely interleaved and no class is linearly separable.",
    "state": {
      "depthSlider": 0,
      "blockStage": "embedding"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "After the first three ViT blocks",
    "narration": "Following the dashed paths up one stage, the cloud spreads slightly but the colors remain thoroughly intermingled: three blocks alone barely untangle the classes.",
    "state": {
      "depthSlider": 1,
      "blockStage": "blocks 1-3"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Mid-network (blocks 4-6)",
    "narration": "By the middle plane, faint same-color neighborhoods start forming, yet most clusters still overlap — separation is emerging but incomplete.",
    "state": {
      "depthSlider": 2,
      "blockStage": "blocks 4-6"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Late blocks (7-9)",
    "narration": "Same-color points begin pulling together into recognizable groups while the dashed trajectories fan outward, showing the largest reorganization happens in the later blocks.",
    "state": {
      "depthSlider": 3,
      "blockStage": "blocks 7-9"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Final representation (top plane)",
    "narration": "On the top plane each class has collapsed into its own compact, single-colored clump — twelve transformer blocks have turned a tangled distribution into a class-separated one.",
    "state": {
      "depthSlider": 4,
      "blockStage": "blocks 10-12"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.72
}

NOTES: Render planes as THREE.PlaneGeometry rotated -PI/2 about X with a transparent/near-white material plus a LineSegments border and grid; enable depthWrite:false on the dashed curve material and sort by y so curves behind a plane are visibly washed out (draw a semi-opaque white plane quad at ~0.75 opacity to get the source's fading effect). Points as a single THREE.Points cloud per plane with per-vertex colors, or instanced circle sprites for crisper edges; update positions from the precomputed per-layer arrays and lerp with the depth slider for smooth transitions. Dashed curves need THREE.LineDashedMaterial with computeLineDistances(); use CatmullRomCurve3 sampled at ~24 points per segment. Hover: raycast against the point cloud, resolve to a class id, then set opacity/color-boost uniforms for all points and curves of that class (and dim others to ~0.08). blockStage highlights the target plane border (thicker, accent stroke) and raises opacity on only the incoming curve segment. Legend drawn as an HTML/CSS overlay pinned to the left of the canvas (or CanvasTexture sprites in world space) so the monospace text stays crisp; keep it non-rotating even while orbiting. Use an OrthographicCamera for the isometric look; clamp orbit polar angle to 25-80 degrees so the stack never flattens.

Follow the interaction plan above.

